<h2>The prosecution&rsquo;s story</h2>
<p>The body of Sharadamma, wife of PW-7, was discovered on 5 September 2013. The prosecution alleged that the appellant, said to be in an illicit relationship with her, suspected her of another affair, strangled her on the night of 4 September 2013, robbed her of her ornaments and concealed the body in a drum. He was arrested on 12 September 2013, and the police claimed the ornaments were recovered from a trunk at his house on his disclosure.</p>

<h2>Four links, each tested and found wanting</h2>
<p>Applying the five golden principles of <em>Sharad Birdhichand Sarda</em>, the Court worked through the chain circumstance by circumstance:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Motive.</strong> Both courts below had treated general village knowledge of an illicit relationship as proof of motive. But no witness deposed that the appellant harboured suspicion or that any quarrel occurred. The Court called the alleged motive &ldquo;a matter of conjecture and surmise&rdquo;.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Last seen.</strong> The two witnesses&rsquo; statements under Section 161 CrPC were recorded on 1 November 2013 &mdash; 58 days after the incident. The trial court rejected their testimony; the High Court restored it without confronting the delay. The Supreme Court sided with the trial court.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Discovery of the body.</strong> The informant who allegedly pointed to the drum was never traced or examined, and neither the inquest nor the post-mortem noted any trace of limestone on the body or clothing &mdash; casting doubt on the discovery narrative itself.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Recovery of ornaments.</strong> One panch witness was never examined, the appraiser gave inconsistent evidence about his signatures, no independent witness supported the disclosure, and the prosecution never proved the deceased was wearing the ornaments when last seen. One witness even said police told him the articles had been found the very next day &mdash; contradicting the claimed recovery eight days later.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Sanwat Khan principle</h2>
<p>The judgment&rsquo;s most practically useful holding concerns what a recovery can and cannot prove. Unexplained possession of a deceased&rsquo;s articles triggers only the presumption in Illustration (a) to Section 114 of the Evidence Act: that the possessor is either the thief or a receiver of stolen property. To travel from theft to murder, the prosecution must prove that the theft and the homicide formed part of the same transaction. Here it could not &mdash; and, tellingly, no charge of theft or robbery had been framed at all. The appellant&rsquo;s acquittal under Section 201 IPC had also attained finality because the State never appealed it, leaving the prosecution in the odd position of a murder conviction standing alone.</p>

<h2>&ldquo;May be&rdquo; is not &ldquo;must be&rdquo;</h2>
<p>Quoting <em>Shivaji Sahabrao Bobade</em>, the Court recalled the mental distance between &ldquo;may have committed the offence&rdquo; and &ldquo;must have committed the offence&rdquo;. A circumstantial case succeeds only when every hypothesis except guilt is excluded. Finding the chain broken at several links, the Court quashed the conviction and the life sentence, acquitted the appellant of the Section 302 IPC charge, and directed his release forthwith unless required in another case.</p>

<h2>What the decision signals for appellate practice</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Attack the links, not the verdict.</strong> Concurrent findings can be reopened under Article 136 where a specific link &mdash; motive, last seen, recovery &mdash; rests on no evidence or ignores a finding the trial court itself recorded.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Delay in Section 161 statements matters.</strong> A two-month gap in recording last-seen witnesses, unexplained, can be fatal; an appellate court restoring such evidence must give reasons for doing so.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Recoveries need scaffolding.</strong> Panch witnesses must be examined, the deceased&rsquo;s possession of the articles must be proved, and the charge framed must match the inference sought.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information only. It is not legal advice and does not comment on any pending proceeding.</div>
